FAIRNESS 10 objection(s) · 2 serious
========================================================================

  [NEW · MINOR · CONTRADICTION] The draft describes the Phase 3 as 'blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried' but the Phase 3's primary endpoint is also investigator-assessed, a fact the draft itself acknowledges two paragraphs later — making the framing in the 'Why the blinding row matters' section internally inconsistent.
     quote         : “It is the more trustworthy trial by a wide margin — seven times larger, and blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried.”
     why           : The draft correctly notes elsewhere that INTerpath-001's primary endpoint is 'recurrence as assessed by the investigator.' Saying the Phase 3 is 'blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried' overstates the correction: the double-blind design reduces directional bias but does not eliminate investigator assessment. The draft's own later paragraph says exactly this. The sentence as written contradicts the body's more careful treatment of the same point, and the changelog even records a prior correction of 'exactly' this language.
     fix           : Change to: 'blinded against the directional pull that assessment bias carried in the open-label trial' — or simply cross-reference the caveat that follows two paragraphs later rather than implying the bias is fully removed.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft calls KEYNOTE-942 'an open-label study of 157 patients, not the blinded study of 1,137 that was announced' — the contrast implies the Phase 3 is blinded in a way that directly addresses the open-label weakness, but the Phase 3's primary endpoint is also investigator-assessed, so the contrast is sharper than the facts support.
     quote         : “the numbers carried in the coverage — the 49% reduction in the hazard of recurrence or death, the 59% reduction in the hazard of distant metastasis or death, both from the five-year follow-up — describe a different trial: an open-label study of 157 patients, not the blinded study of 1,137 that was announced.”
     why           : Calling INTerpath-001 simply 'the blinded study' in direct contrast to the 'open-label' Phase 2b implies the blinding fully resolves the assessment-bias problem. The draft's own later section acknowledges the Phase 3 endpoint is still investigator-assessed. The short version, read alone, leaves a misleading impression that the two trials differ on this dimension more completely than they do.
     fix           : Add a parenthetical: 'the double-blind study of 1,137 that was announced (whose primary endpoint is also investigator-assessed, though the blinding substantially limits directional bias).'

  [NEW · MINOR · THIRD_PARTY] The draft attributes a specific argument to Morning Glory Sciences — that absolute recurrence risk is lower in stage IIB/IIC patients so 'the same hazard ratio delivers a smaller absolute benefit' — but then describes Morning Glory Sciences as 'the English edition of a non-English industry site, which we cite for an argument we have checked ourselves rather than on its standing,' which is an unsupported characterisation of the outlet's nature and standing.
     quote         : “Morning Glory Sciences — the English edition of a non-English industry site, which we cite for an argument we have checked ourselves rather than on its standing”
     why           : Describing Morning Glory Sciences as a 'non-English industry site' whose standing is insufficient to cite on its own is a characterisation of a third party's credibility and editorial nature. No evidence is offered for this description. If Morning Glory Sciences is a legitimate specialist outlet, this framing is unfair to them and potentially misleading to readers about the quality of the source.
     fix           : Either remove the parenthetical characterisation of the outlet's standing entirely, or replace it with a factual description that can be verified — e.g. its country of origin and publication type — without the editorial judgment about its standing.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft states the Phase 3 trial enrolled 'Stage IIB–IV' patients and that the upper end 'did not move: stage IV was in both,' but the KEYNOTE-942 registry and Lancet paper describe its population as stage IIIB–IV, not stage III–IV, meaning the upper bound comparison is correct but the characterisation of what 'did not move' should specify IIIB–IV to IV, not III–IV.
     quote         : “The upper end did not move: stage IV was in both.”
     why           : This sentence is accurate as far as it goes — stage IV was indeed in both trials. However, the surrounding sentence says the Phase 3 'widened it downward — adding IIB, IIC and IIIA below the earlier trial's floor of IIIB.' IIIA is listed as newly added, which is correct per the registry (KEYNOTE-942 enrolled IIIB–IV; INTerpath-001 enrolled IIB–IV including IIIA). The sentence about the upper end not moving is fine, but the claim that IIIA is newly added deserves a source check — the ASCO abstract for INTerpath-001 confirms stage IIB, IIC, III, or IV, and KEYNOTE-942 enrolled IIIB–IV, so IIIA is indeed newly included. This is accurate and not an error, but the phrasing 'the upper end did not move' could be read as implying stage III was in both, when KEYNOTE-942's floor was IIIB. This is a minor precision issue, not a material error.
     fix           : No change required; the sentence is accurate. Flag resolved.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · FACT] The draft states the five-year topline of 20 January 2026 reporting a one-sided nominal p = 0.0075 'is still in nothing we hold' and 'stays out' — but CancerNetwork and Targeted Oncology both report this figure (HR 0.510; 1-sided nominal P = 0.0075) attributed to a Merck press release of January 20, 2026, which is publicly available and cited in specialist coverage the draft itself holds.
     quote         : “We also removed a 'five-year topline of 20 January 2026' reporting a one-sided nominal p = 0.0075. That document is still in nothing we hold and in no source list on this page, and nothing found since has changed that. It stays out until somebody produces it.”
     why           : The p = 0.0075 figure appears in CancerNetwork (citing 'Merck. January 20, 2026') and in Targeted Oncology, both of which are specialist outlets the draft holds or cites. The draft's own source list includes the ASCO Post's coverage of the five-year data. The January 20, 2026 Merck press release is publicly available and cited in the specialist literature. Saying the figure 'exists in nothing we hold' and keeping it off the page when it is verifiable from sources the draft already uses is an error of omission that could mislead readers about the evidentiary basis for the p-value, and it contradicts the draft's own standard of tracing every number to a company release or peer-reviewed paper.
     fix           : Obtain the January 20, 2026 Merck press release (publicly available; cited by CancerNetwork and Targeted Oncology), add it to the source list, restore the one-sided nominal p = 0.0075 to the page with correct attribution, and remove the correction notice claiming the figure is unverifiable.

  [NEW · MINOR · CONTRADICTION] The draft says 'the release says so' when describing that the hazard ratios in the announcement belong to KEYNOTE-942 — this is accurate — but then says 'No hazard ratio, no confidence interval, no p-value and no percentage describing the size of that effect' for the Phase 3, which is correct, yet the scorecard section says 'data support scores 1 — the rubric's anchor for 1 is purely qualitative assertion with no numeric support' — the Phase 3 announcement does contain the qualitative result that both endpoints were met as 'statistically significant and clinically meaningful,' which is more than purely qualitative assertion with no numeric support, even if no effect size is given.
     quote         : “Data support scores 1 — the rubric's anchor for 1 is purely qualitative assertion with no numeric support, which is what a statement that two endpoints were met, with no figure for either, is.”
     why           : The draft's own changelog records a prior correction: 'We had scored data support 1 against the rubric's anchor of "no data cited," when a directional result was cited and what is absent is magnitude.' The current text still equates 'met its endpoints' with 'purely qualitative assertion with no numeric support.' A threshold crossing at a prespecified interim analysis is a directional result with implicit statistical content (the threshold was crossed), not a purely qualitative assertion. The draft's own earlier correction acknowledged this distinction and then re-introduced the same conflation.
     fix           : Revise to: 'Data support scores 1 because the rubric's lowest anchor applies when no effect size — no hazard ratio, interval, or percentage — has been released; a statement that a threshold was crossed is directional but carries no magnitude, and magnitude is what this dimension measures.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says 'Most specialist outlets attributed the earlier trial's figures correctly' and then names six outlets — but it also says 'An earlier version of this sentence named OncLive and FierceBiotech when we held neither' and that FierceBiotech 'we still do not hold' — meaning the claim that 'most' outlets attributed correctly is based on a sample that excluded at least one outlet (FierceBiotech) the draft acknowledges it cannot assess.
     quote         : “Every outlet whose article we hold attributed those figures correctly to KEYNOTE-942 — The ASCO Post, Dermatology Times, Practical Dermatology, KOL Pulse, MLQ News and OncLive”
     why           : The claim 'every outlet whose article we hold' is accurate and appropriately scoped. However, the lead sentence of the section says 'Most specialist outlets attributed the earlier trial's figures correctly' — a broader claim than 'every outlet we hold.' The draft cannot establish what outlets it does not hold did. 'Most' implies a representative sample; the sample is self-selected by what the publication could obtain.
     fix           : Change 'Most specialist outlets' to 'Every specialist outlet whose coverage we hold' to match the scoped claim that follows.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says the Phase 3 is 'double-blind, placebo-controlled, so nobody assessing a recurrence knows which treatment produced it' — but the Phase 3 is placebo- AND active-comparator-controlled, meaning the comparison arm receives pembrolizumab plus placebo, not pembrolizumab alone; the blinding is between vaccine and placebo, not between treatment and no treatment.
     quote         : “It is double-blind and placebo-controlled, so nobody assessing a recurrence knows which treatment produced it.”
     why           : This is accurate in its essential claim — the assessor is blinded — but 'placebo-controlled' alone understates the design. The trial is placebo- and active-comparator-controlled: both arms receive pembrolizumab; the experimental arm adds intismeran and the control arm adds placebo. The sentence is not wrong but is incomplete in a way that could mislead a reader into thinking the control arm receives no active treatment, which matters for interpreting the result.
     fix           : Change to: 'It is double-blind and placebo-controlled — both arms receive pembrolizumab; the experimental arm adds intismeran and the control arm adds matched placebo — so nobody assessing a recurrence knows which treatment produced it.'

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CALIBRATION] The draft's 'What is established' section states 'Any-grade immune-related adverse events were similar — 45.2% on the combination versus 44% on pembrolizumab alone' and then says 'Reading only the first pair would tell you the added therapy costs nothing, and it does' — the final clause 'and it does' is ambiguous and could be read as saying the added therapy does cost nothing, the opposite of the intended meaning.
     quote         : “Reading only the first pair would tell you the added therapy costs nothing, and it does.”
     why           : The sentence is grammatically ambiguous. 'And it does' most naturally reads as confirming the preceding clause — i.e., 'the added therapy costs nothing' — which is the opposite of what the draft intends. The intended meaning is that the added therapy does carry a cost (grade 3+ events at 25% vs 18%). A reader who stops at this sentence takes away the wrong conclusion about safety.
     fix           : Rewrite as: 'Reading only the first pair would tell you the added therapy costs nothing in toxicity — but it does carry a cost: grade 3 or worse events were higher.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft describes the scoring formula for 'Is the effect real?' as '(3×.25 + 4×.20 + 4×.15 + 5×.10 + 5×.10) ÷ .80' but the six dimensions listed are Source quality 3, Data support 1, Reproducibility 4, Consensus 4, Recency 5, Rigor 5 — the formula omits Data support (weight .20) from the numerator and the denominator is .80 rather than 1.0, which implies Data support is excluded from this sub-score, but the text does not say so explicitly in the formula line itself.
     quote         : “(3×.25 + 4×.20 + 4×.15 + 5×.10 + 5×.10) ÷ .80”
     why           : The formula is internally consistent — five dimensions at weights .25+.20+.15+.10+.10 = .80, with Data support excluded and scored separately — but a reader checking the arithmetic will find only five terms in the numerator against six listed dimensions and no explanation within the formula line of why Data support (scored 1) is absent. The rubric section says 'only one of the six dimensions, data support, asks whether an effect size exists at all, so the magnitude score rests on that dimension alone' but this explanation appears after the formula, not beside it.
     fix           : Add a parenthetical immediately after the formula: '(Data support excluded from this sub-score; it is the sole input to the magnitude score below.)'

