  Morning Glory Sciences
     got right     : Explicitly stated that the Phase 2b was open-label in stage IIIB–IV, that the Phase 3 adds stage IIB/IIC node-negative disease, that the Phase 3 HRs have not been disclosed and therefore the 157-patient figures 'cannot be placed alongside the 1,137-patient result' and 'belong to the Phase 2b population, not to this one.' Also noted the double-blind design eliminates open-label assessment bias as an explanation for the directional result, while correctly flagging that magnitude cannot be assessed.
     still omitted : Does not explain the width of the OS confidence interval (0.165–1.345) or the n=14 death count underlying it, does not discuss the one-sided vs two-sided p-value framing, and does not give the absolute RFS or OS landmark rates that would let a reader feel the size of the Phase 2b effect.
     https://www.morningglorysciences.com/en/intismeran-interpath-001-phase3-melanoma-2026-en/

  Practical Dermatology
     got right     : Stated plainly and in its own words that 'The companies did not disclose hazard ratios, absolute event rates, P values, or other phase 3 efficacy estimates in the topline announcement.' Attributed the 49%/59% figures to the KEYNOTE-942 five-year follow-up. Noted the trial will continue to evaluate OS.
     still omitted : No discussion of the OS confidence interval width or the n=14 death count, no explanation of what a hazard ratio measures versus a headcount, no note on the open-label design of the Phase 2b versus the blinded Phase 3, and no discussion of the population difference (stage IIB/IIC newly included).
     https://practicaldermatology.com/news/intismeran-plus-pembrolizumab-meets-dual-phase-3-melanoma-endpoints/2488176/

  Dermatology Times
     got right     : Stated that 'Detailed phase 3 efficacy metrics (e.g., HRs) were not disclosed; OS and other secondary endpoints remain immature.' Attributed the 49%/59% figures to the Phase 2b KEYNOTE-942 five-year data. Correctly described the trial as double-blind with 1,137 patients randomised 2:1.
     still omitted : No discussion of the OS interval width or the death count behind it, no explanation of the open-label versus blinded design difference between the two trials, no note on the population expansion to stage IIB/IIC, and no discussion of what 'met its endpoint' does and does not convey about effect size.
     https://www.dermatologytimes.com/view/personalized-mrna-based-melanoma-vaccine-meets-primary-endpoints-in-landmark-phase-3-trial

  MLQ News
     got right     : Stated that 'The companies have not disclosed the Phase 3 hazard ratios, p-values, median follow-up or detailed adverse-event rates.' Noted that the stock moves 'came before the companies disclosed the detailed efficacy and safety data needed for regulatory, clinical and payer review.' Cited an ASCO discussant (Grant McArthur) who said Phase 2 results alone were insufficient to change clinical practice and noted the Phase 3 covers a broader population including stage II disease.
     still omitted : Does not enumerate confidence intervals or event counts as missing (the draft's quoted version of the MLQ sentence includes these; the actual text does not). No discussion of the OS interval width, the n=14 death count, the open-label versus blinded design distinction, or the one-sided/two-sided p-value issue.
     https://mlq.ai/news/moderna-mercks-personalized-melanoma-therapy-meets-two-phase-3-endpoints/

  OncoDaily
     got right     : Explicitly listed what was not disclosed: 'The Phase 3 hazard ratios, confidence intervals, absolute RFS and DMFS rates, subgroup analyses, and detailed safety results have not yet been reported.' Stated that OS 'does not yet demonstrate an overall survival benefit' and that 'longer follow-up will be required to determine whether the reduction in recurrence and distant metastasis ultimately translates into improved survival.' Noted that interpretation will require assessment of absolute benefit, toxicity, discontinuation, and quality of life — not just relative RFS/DMFS. Correctly attributed the 49%/59% figures to the Phase 2b.
     still omitted : Does not explain the OS confidence interval width or the n=14 death count, does not discuss the open-label design of the Phase 2b as a limitation on the figures being cited, and does not address the one-sided/two-sided p-value distinction or the population difference between the two trials.
